[B]The "test before you trust" update.[/B] Changing enchantment rules on a live server has always been a leap of faith: you add a ban, hit reload, and find out from your players what it actually did. Not anymore.

[SIZE=4][COLOR=#9B59B6][B]Dry-run mode — see it before it happens[/B][/COLOR][/SIZE]
Set [ICODE]dry-run: true[/ICODE] and AntiEnchants switches to observe-only: it detects every violation exactly as if it were live, logs what it [I]would[/I] strip or cap (console, plus [ICODE]strips.log[/ICODE] if the audit log is on) — and doesn't touch a single item.
[LIST]
[*]Thinking about banning Fortune? Turn dry-run on, watch the logs for a day, and know precisely how many players are affected and what they'd lose — before anyone loses anything.
[*]Everything stands down, not just the strips: enchanting tables, anvils, grindstones, smithing tables, villager trades and mending all behave like vanilla while you observe.
[*]Repeated findings are deduplicated, so a player clicking around their inventory doesn't flood your console with the same report.
[*]Impossible to forget: a loud warning on startup and reload, a banner in [ICODE]/ae list[/ICODE], and joining admins get reminded in chat that the plugin is observing, not enforcing.
[/LIST]

[SIZE=4][COLOR=#9B59B6][B]/ae purge — apply the rules NOW[/B][/COLOR][/SIZE]
Until now, a new rule reached each player whenever they next joined or clicked their inventory. [ICODE]/ae purge all[/ICODE] cleans every online inventory the moment you decide:
[LIST]
[*][ICODE]/ae purge <player>[/ICODE] for one player, [ICODE]/ae purge all[/ICODE] for everyone online, with a summary of how many enchantments were removed and capped.
[*]It runs the exact same pipeline as the automatic strips — bypass permissions, player messages, compensation and the audit log all apply.
[*]With dry-run on, it reports instead of cleaning: [ICODE]dry-run: true[/ICODE] + [ICODE]/ae purge all[/ICODE] = an instant, zero-risk impact report of your current rules across the whole server.
[/LIST]

The two are built to be used together: preview with dry-run, tune with [ICODE]/ae add[/ICODE]/[ICODE]remove[/ICODE]/[ICODE]cap[/ICODE], flip dry-run off, then [ICODE]/ae purge all[/ICODE] to apply the cleanup in one go — on your schedule, not the plugin's.

[SIZE=4][COLOR=#9B59B6][B]A hole plugged and a sharper magnifying glass[/B][/COLOR][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*][B]Smithing table covered[/B] — netherite upgrades carry every enchantment onto the result, so a banned or over-cap enchant could ride a diamond-to-netherite upgrade straight past the anvil block. [ICODE]block-at-smithing[/ICODE] (on by default) cleans the smithing preview like the anvil and grindstone ones.
[*][ICODE]/ae check <player>[/ICODE] — point the inspector at someone else: every slot of their inventory (armour and offhand included), each violating item listed with exactly what's banned or over the cap. Works from console — settle a report without logging in.
[/LIST]

[SIZE=4][COLOR=#9B59B6][B]Housekeeping[/B][/COLOR][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*]The new options ([ICODE]dry-run[/ICODE], [ICODE]block-at-smithing[/ICODE]) are added to your existing config automatically, comments included; your edited values stay untouched. Upgrade path covered by automated tests, as always.
[*][ICODE]/ae purge[/ICODE] and [ICODE]/ae check[/ICODE] tab-complete online player names, and dry-run status is shown in [ICODE]/ae list[/ICODE] and after [ICODE]/ae reload[/ICODE].
[/LIST]

No breaking changes: drop the jar in and your old config keeps working — and keeps enforcing, exactly as before.
